    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how a capacitor works in an ac circuit compared to dc? specifically why does it block dc but let ac through? also what is the formula for capacitive reactance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! In an AC circuit, a capacitor allows alternating current (AC) to pass through because the changing direction of the current continually reverses the polarity of the voltage across the capacitor, allowing the charges to move in and out efficiently. DC, on the other hand, once the charges are fully inverted, doesn't reverse, preventing further current through the capacitor. The formula for capacitive reactance is \\( X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi fC} \\), where \\( X_C \\) is the capacitive reactance, \\( f \\) is the frequency, and \\( C \\) is the capacitance.",
